{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_13-42-19-etcd_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nu={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Interpret the retained execution of the revised removed-singleton-leader probe (CheckRun bc8372740399467686bed25385e51001, fixed experiment 8f8a7578765146509956132f0183a8c3-experiment, version 2): six parsed events without loss, four comparisons hold and the witness comparison is violated, so the question moves from a source-grounded suspicion to an evidence-backed account of a stale, unconfirmed ReadIndex answer on a node that is no longer the configuration's voter.\\\",\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[{\n  \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"9ad1a10e85bb41578fbb8a42a6890ab3-question-0\\\",\n  \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"8f8a7578765146509956132f0183a8c3-experiment\\\",\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"tracker-singleton\\\",\\\"readindex-singleton-branch\\\",\\\"prop-removed-leader\\\",\\\"switchtoconfig-removal\\\",\\\"config-stepdown-doc\\\",\\\"readonlysafe-doc\\\",\\\"node-readindex-contract\\\",\\\"readme-linearizable\\\"],\n  \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"bc8372740399467686bed25385e51001\\\":[1,2,3,4,5]},\n  \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n  \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n  \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"The account now rests on this receipt. Source side: ProgressTracker.IsSingleton (tracker/tracker.go:158-162) is a size test - one member in Voters[0], none in Voters[1] - and its comment asserts that the single voting member is the leader, yet nothing compares that member with the local node id; stepLeader's MsgReadIndex case (raft.go:1354-1361) treats the test as authority to answer locally from r.raftLog.committed, as a ReadState for a local request or MsgReadIndexResp for a forwarded one, without any heartbeat or readOnly round, while the documented ReadOnlySafe contract (raft.go:233-244) and the ReadIndex contract (node.go:218-224, README.md:27-32) promise a linearizable read once the application advances past the returned index. The library reaches the offending state through its own permitted use: applying a committed change that removes the leading node leaves it leading under the default Config.StepDownOnRemoval=false (raft.go:1985-2012, raft.go:282-287) with a configuration whose only voter is another node, and the same library refuses that node's proposals on the write path (raft.go:1294-1303) but not its read answers. Executed interval (parsed indices): index 1, after the application applied the removal, node 1 is StateLeader at term 1 with config voters=(2), singleton true, is_voter false, its own commit index frozen at 2; index 2, node 2 leads the reduced configuration at term 2 with committed index 4 while the removed leader is at 2, so an entry the removed leader never receives is already committed; index 3, the ReadIndex request answered by the removed leader produced read_states 1 with read_index 2, i.e. below the commit index 4 the actual leader already had, with heartbeats_emitted 0, messages_emitted empty, pending_reads 0 and unconfirmed_reads 0 - the answer was produced locally by the size shortcut, with no contact with the current configuration at all (this comparison is the violated witness); index 4, the same request answered by node 2, the actual leader, returned read_index 4, covering its commit index, so the stale answer is specific to the node that is no longer the voter; index 5, after three heartbeat intervals with no replies from the current configuration the removed leader is still leading at term 1 and repeated read_index 2 for a second request, so nothing in the periodic path repairs the answer. The scope of the finding: for each such request the application is licensed to serve state that may miss an entry committed before the read was issued, and re-issuing the request against the same node reproduces the same index instead of repairing it. The finding does not claim that raft can detect staleness in general, and it does not claim that the size shortcut is wrong when the single voter is the local node - the defect is that the shortcut is taken from a test that establishes the size of the configuration while the answer depends on being that configuration's voter.\\\",\n  \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n   \\\"The control comparison holds (parsed index 4): the same request answered by the node that is the actual leader returns an index covering its commit index, so the local singleton answer is not stale by construction - the violation is produced by the identity of the answering node, not by answering locally.\\\",\n   \\\"A client is expected to direct linearizable reads at the current leader, and a node that was removed is a client-side premise the library cannot generally check: a removed or partitioned leader also answers old-term requests until a higher term reaches it, which this shortcut does not cause.\\\",\n   \\\"The reachable window depends on the harness's stated configuration (StepDownOnRemoval false, CheckQuorum and PreVote off, so nothing carries the higher term back to the removed leader); the receipt does not measure a window with those options enabled.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"limitations\\\":[\n   \\\"The scenario requires the application to apply a committed configuration change that removes the leading node while Config.StepDownOnRemoval is false; applications that set that option, or that stop the removed node, do not reach it.\\\",\n   \\\"The witness is at the ReadIndex contract level: the receipt shows the returned index is below an already-committed entry and that no confirmation message was sent. No state machine was run, so the miss of that write in a served value is a consequence of the contract, not something the receipt measures.\\\",\n   \\\"Only the local request path was exercised; the forwarded form (a follower's MsgReadIndex answered with MsgReadIndexResp) rests on the same shortcut (raft.go:1354-1361) but was not measured here.\\\",\n   \\\"The removed leader stops answering this way as soon as a higher term reaches it, which with CheckQuorum or PreVote happens on the next heartbeat exchange; the receipt shows persistence only for the exercised configuration, and no rate or duration bound is claimed.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"knowledge\\\":[\n   {\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"9ad1a10e85bb41578fbb8a42a6890ab3-knowledge-0\\\",\\\"revision\\\":1,\\\"assertion\\\":\\\"IsSingleton is a size-only test over the configuration and never compares the single voter with the local node.\\\"},\n   {\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"9ad1a10e85bb41578fbb8a42a6890ab3-knowledge-1\\\",\\\"revision\\\":1,\\\"assertion\\\":\\\"StepLeader answers MsgReadIndex locally whenever IsSingleton holds, using its own commit index and without a round.\\\"},\n   {\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"9ad1a10e85bb41578fbb8a42a6890ab3-knowledge-2\\\",\\\"revision\\\":1,\\\"assertion\\\":\\\"A removed leader keeps leading by default while its proposals are refused and its read answers are not.\\\"}\n  ]}]}\nopen('update5.json','w').write(json.dumps(u,indent=2)+\\\"\\\\n\\\")\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_13-42-19-etcd_raft-real-run --submission update5.json 2>&1 | tail -6\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
